WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.680
The Dude abided once, so why&nbsp;
hasn't he abided again? We're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:07.140
just dropping in to see what&nbsp;
condition his sequel is in.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:12.540
Not only did Joel and Ethan Coen write and direct&nbsp;
some of the most critically acclaimed films of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:18.480
the 1990s and 2000s, but they made a lot of them,&nbsp;
quickly. After the release of "The Big Lebowski"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:24.480
in 1998, the duo churned out 11 movies over the&nbsp;
next 20 years. However, one thing they've never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:29.580
done — and might never do — is a sequel to any&nbsp;
of their works, especially "The Big Lebowski."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:33.840
It is worth noting, however, that there was&nbsp;
a theoretical "Barton Fink" follow-up they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:39.120
kicked around called "Old Fink," so a sequel&nbsp;
is not entirely impossible… just unlikely.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:44.460
So what's the resistance here? According to&nbsp;
the Toronto Star, when asked about another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:49.370
"Big Lebowski" movie at the 2013 Cannes&nbsp;
Film Festival, Joel Coen told reporters,

00:00:49.439 --> 00:00:51.179
"I just don't like sequels."

00:00:51.179 --> 00:00:54.645
To seemingly put a nail in that&nbsp;
coffin, Ethan Coen admitted,

00:00:54.976 --> 00:00:56.836
"I don't see it in our future."

00:00:56.836 --> 00:00:58.226
"This will not stand."

00:00:58.226 --> 00:01:02.220
Further complicating a proper start on the&nbsp;
"Big Lebowski" sequel is the fact that Joel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:06.840
and Ethan Coen have entered a new phase&nbsp;
of their working relationship. In 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:11.460
Joel went solo and directed "The Tragedy&nbsp;
of Macbeth." Ethan followed suit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:14.546
making "Drive-Away Dolls"&nbsp;
with his wife, Tricia Cooke.

00:01:14.546 --> 00:01:18.120
However, it was recently revealed that&nbsp;
the Coens haven't permanently ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:22.928
their cinematic partnership. In the&nbsp;
August 2023 issue of Empire Magazine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.928 --> 00:01:27.780
Ethan revealed that he and Joel are working on&nbsp;
a new project together. Chances are almost zero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:32.400
that it's a sequel to "The Big Lebowski," but if&nbsp;
the brothers are making movies together again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.468
perhaps one day we might get&nbsp;
more bowling alley adventures.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:40.440
Needless to say, Jeff Bridges is totally down&nbsp;
for "The Big Lebowski 2." When People asked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:45.109
about the possibility of a follow-up film&nbsp;
in May 2023, the legendary actor replied,

00:01:45.109 --> 00:01:49.200
"Oh god! If the brothers were involved,&nbsp;
I certainly would. The brothers, they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:53.400
mysterious and full of surprises. [...] I don't&nbsp;
think they'll make a sequel. But like I say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:57.183
they're surprising, so maybe they'll&nbsp;
surprise me and make a sequel."

00:01:57.183 --> 00:02:02.400
But what about the rest of the cast? Well, in&nbsp;
February 2011, Tara Reid — who played kidnapping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:07.380
"victim" Bunny Lebowski — told Hollywood TV about&nbsp;
all the future projects she had lined up and said:

00:02:07.807 --> 00:02:11.527
"We'll be doing 'American Pie 4' this&nbsp;
year and 'Big Lebowski 2' this year."

00:02:11.527 --> 00:02:16.320
Of course, there was one major problem with that&nbsp;
statement — there was no such "Lebowski" sequel in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:21.304
development. Upon hearing this, a blindsided Ethan&nbsp;
Coen quipped to the Austin American-Statesman,

00:02:21.373 --> 00:02:23.113
"I'm glad she's working on it."

00:02:23.113 --> 00:02:27.480
An employee of Reid's told Entertainment Weekly&nbsp;
that their boss was likely confused by comments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:31.860
Jeff Bridges made a few months earlier. He had&nbsp;
publicly advocated for a new "Lebowski" movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:35.482
but he'd also admitted that such a&nbsp;
film wasn't actually in the works.

00:02:35.482 --> 00:02:40.020
Still, Reid appears to be on board, and she and&nbsp;
Bridges aren't the only ones who'd be down to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:43.680
another crack at their kooky characters.&nbsp;
Sam Elliott — who played the Stranger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:47.340
the sarsaparilla-drinking narrator&nbsp;
and passive participant in "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:51.000
Big Lebowski" — would also very much&nbsp;
like to film a follow-up. When asked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:55.124
by Variety in 2017 if he'd be willing&nbsp;
to make the sequel, Elliott replied,

00:02:55.124 --> 00:02:56.444
"In a heartbeat."

00:02:56.444 --> 00:02:59.940
In 2014, some Coen fans believed&nbsp;
they were going to get what they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:03.240
always wanted. In September of that&nbsp;
year, The National Report claimed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:06.840
that Joel and Ethan Coen had confirmed&nbsp;
shooting would begin shortly on a "Big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:12.000
Lebowski" sequel. The report also included an&nbsp;
alleged statement from Ethan Coen that read,

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.040
"We're thrilled to be coming back to film a second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:17.880
part to this classic movie. For years we've&nbsp;
been staying away from doing this project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:22.402
but when we received this new script and the&nbsp;
cast fell into place, it was a no-brainer."

00:03:22.402 --> 00:03:27.420
According to Snopes, the story spread fast on&nbsp;
social media and other far-reaching websites&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:32.040
before the general public realized it wasn't&nbsp;
true. And considering how the Coen brothers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:36.720
almost exclusively pen the movies they direct,&nbsp;
the part about receiving a new script should've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:40.560
been a dead giveaway. As it turns out,&nbsp;
The National Report was a faux-news site,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:45.778
and it even included a disclaimer alerting&nbsp;
readers to the "fictitious" nature of its stories.

00:03:45.778 --> 00:03:49.920
While there are no official concepts for a&nbsp;
true sequel to "The Big Lebowski," this hasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:54.120
stopped interested parties from speculating&nbsp;
on the plot of such a project — including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:58.260
the Dude himself. "Lebowski" star Jeff&nbsp;
Bridges wants the Coens to pursue a film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:01.980
about his character's offspring.&nbsp;
He told Business Insider in 2016,

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:06.360
"I'm hoping they make a little Lebowski&nbsp;
because it's all set up. I impregnated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:10.319
Maude. As the Stranger says, 'There's a&nbsp;
little Lebowski on the way,' you know?"

00:04:10.319 --> 00:04:12.239
"It really tied the room together."

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:16.620
While no officially sanctioned sequel to&nbsp;
"The Big Lebowski" has ever been greenlit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:20.520
written, produced, or even approved&nbsp;
by the original film's creators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:23.820
the movie has inspired some&nbsp;
low-key and unconnected spinoffs.

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:29.040
Jeff Lebowski drinks White Russian cocktails&nbsp;
as a matter of course, but in a 2019 Super&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:34.020
Bowl commercial for Stella Artois, he tried one of&nbsp;
the company's beers and enjoyed it. Jeff Bridges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:38.340
reprised his role of the Dude for the ad, and&nbsp;
he even wore the character's memorable costume.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:42.480
Of course, there's also a feature-length film&nbsp;
set in the world of "The Big Lebowski" — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:46.800
little indie flick titled "The Jesus Rolls"&nbsp;
— though the Coen brothers had nothing to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:51.240
with making it. Instead, the 2019 film&nbsp;
was written and directed by actor John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:56.520
Turturro as a way to once again portray his&nbsp;
"Lebowski" character — convicted sex offender&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:01.560
and foul-mouthed master bowler Jesus Quintana.&nbsp;
"The Jesus" is the only connection to "The Big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:06.000
Lebowski," as the film is otherwise about the&nbsp;
character's post-prison release crime spree,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:11.220
and it actually borrows more from the French film&nbsp;
"Going Places" than its Coen brothers predecessor.

